.TH sort "" "Command"
.SH NAME
sort \- sort lines of text
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBsort [\fIoptions\fB] [ [+\fIbeg\fB [\-\fIend\fB] ] [\fIfile ...\fB] ]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
sort -- sort lines of text
Usage:	sort [ options ] [ [ +beg [ -end ] ] [ file ... ]
Options:
	-c	Check if input is already ordered
	-m	Merge already-sorted input files
	-o name	Place output on file 'name' rather than standard output
	-tc	Tab character is 'c'
	-T dir	Use directory 'dir' for temporary files
	-u	Only output records for which keys are unique
Key options:
	-b	Skip leading blanks in fields
	-d	Dictionary ordering for keys
	-f	Fold upper case into lower case in key comparison
	-i	Ignore control characters in key comparison
	-n	Numeric comparison
	-r	Reverse sort ordering
Position:
	+m.nf	Key starts 'm' fields into record and 'n' characters
		into that field;  'f' may contain optional flags from
		key options above which apply only to that positional
	-m.nf	Optional ending position of key (same form as above)
If no 'file' is given, the standard input is sorted.
.HE
.B sort
reads lines from each
.IR file ,
or from the standard input if none is given, sorts them, and writes
the result to the standard output.
By default the sort key is the entire line and lines are compared
byte for byte.
.PP
.B sort
does its work with a polyphase merge: it first reads the input into
temporary files, using replacement selection to build large initial
runs, and then repeatedly merges those runs together in the temporary
files until one run remains, which becomes the output.
.PP
A key field is selected by one or two
.I position
arguments,
\fB+\fIm\fB.\fInf\fR
and
\fB\-\fIm\fB.\fInf\fR.
.I m
is the number of fields to skip and
.I n
the number of characters to skip within the following field before
the key starts (for
.BR \-m.nf ,
before it ends); fields are separated by blanks and tabs, or by the
character set with
.BR \-t ,
and leading separators before a field are also skipped once
.I m
is nonzero.
.I f
may contain any of the key-ordering letters
.B b d f i n r
listed below; these apply only to this one key, overriding the
global ordering options for it.
If no position is given, the key is the whole line.
More than one
\fB+\fIm.nf\fB \fR[\fB\-\fIm.nf\fB]\fR
pair may be given, and are applied in order until one pair compares
unequal.
.PP
Options:
.IP \fB\-b\fR
Skip leading blanks (spaces and tabs) when locating and comparing key
fields.
.IP \fB\-d\fR
Dictionary order: compare keys using only letters, digits, and blanks,
ignoring all other characters.
.IP \fB\-f\fR
Fold upper-case letters to lower case for comparison.
.IP \fB\-i\fR
Ignore non-printable characters in key comparisons.
.IP \fB\-n\fR
Compare the key as a signed number (an optional sign, digits, and an
optional decimal point and fraction) rather than as text.
.IP \fB\-r\fR
Reverse the sense of the comparison.
.IP \fB\-c\fR
Check whether the input is already sorted according to the other
options; if not, report the first out-of-order line and exit with a
nonzero status. No output is produced, and
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-o\fR, and \fB\-u\fR
are ignored.
.IP \fB\-m\fR
Merge the input files, which are each assumed to already be sorted;
this avoids the initial run-forming pass and is faster on large,
already-sorted input.
.IP "\fB\-o \fIname\fR"
Write the output to
.I name
instead of the standard output; this may safely be one of the input
files.
.IP \fB\-t\fIc\fR
Use the single character
.I c
as the field separator, instead of runs of blanks and tabs.
.IP "\fB\-T \fIdir\fR"
Create temporary files in
.I dir
instead of the default location.
.IP \fB\-u\fR
Output only one line for each set of lines that compare equal on the
key; later duplicates are discarded.
.PP
Key-ordering letters given directly on the command line (not attached
to a
.B +\fIm.nf\fR
position) set the default ordering for the whole line.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 20n
\fB/tmp/sort\fIpid\fBx\fR	temporary run and merge files
\fB/usr/tmp/sort\fIpid\fBx\fR	used if \fB/tmp\fR is not writable
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B uniq
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B sort
exits with a nonzero status if a temporary file could not be created
or written, if an input file could not be opened, if an input record
is too long, or, with
.BR \-c ,
if the input was not correctly ordered.
.SH NOTES
An input or key record may not exceed 400 characters;
.B sort
aborts if this limit is exceeded.
.PP
Without
.B \-T
.B sort
first tries
.B /tmp
for its temporary files and, if that fails, falls back to
.BR /usr/tmp .
